  "answer_text": "In Massachusetts, the maximum amount you can collect for unemployment depends on whether you are referring to standard **Unemployment Insurance (UI)** or **Paid Family and Medical Leave (PFML)**. \n\n### 1. Standard Unemployment Insurance (UI)\nAs of the latest rate adjustments, the limits for standard unemployment benefits are as follows:\n\n* **Maximum Weekly Benefit:** **$1,105 per week** (excluding dependency allowance). *(Note: The state Department of Unemployment Assistance reviews and updates this rate annually, with the next adjustment scheduled for October 2026).*\n* **Maximum Duration:** Up to **30 weeks**. Massachusetts extends its benefit period from 26 weeks to 30 weeks when unemployment rates in certain local metropolitan areas rise above 5.1%.\n* **Maximum Total Standard Benefit:** **$33,150** ($1,105 \u00d7 30 weeks). However, your actual total benefit credit is capped at the lesser of 30 times your weekly benefit amount *or* 36% of your total wages earned during your \"base period\" (the lookback period of your employment history).\n* **Dependency Allowance:** If you provide more than 50% of the support for dependent children, you may receive an additional **$25 per child, per week**. This dependency allowance is capped at a maximum of 50% of your base weekly benefit rate ($552.50 at the maximum rate).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Paid Family and Medical Leave (PFML)\nSome residents confuse standard unemployment with Massachusetts' separate Paid Family and Medical Leave program. If you are taking leave to care for a family member, bond with a child, or recover from a medical condition:\n\n* **Maximum Weekly Benefit:** **$1,230.39 per week**. \n* **Maximum Duration:** Up to **26 weeks** depending on the qualifying reason (typically 20 weeks for your own serious health condition, 12 weeks for bonding or family care, or 26 weeks to care for an injured service member).",
